<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Cacicazgo of Marién</h1><p class="st-meta">1200 CE – 1497 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Taíno chiefdom of north-western Hispaniola whose cacique Guacanagarí befriended Columbus in 1492 and hosted the first Spanish fort.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Marién occupied the north-western part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, today's northern <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-empire-of-haiti">Haiti</a>. It was one of the island's five principal chiefdoms when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> arrived in December 1492, rooted in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> society of the preceding centuries.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its cacique <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guacanagari">Guacanagarí</a> helped the Spanish salvage the wreck of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-ship">Santa María</a> on Christmas Day 1492, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> built the fort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a> with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timber">timbers</a> in his territory. Marién's people lived in villages with plazas, farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cassava">cassava</a> and fished, and its chief ranked with the other great caciques of the island.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a> was destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caonabo">Caonabo of Maguana</a> in 1493. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guacanagari">Guacanagarí</a> remained allied with the Spanish but saw his people subjected to tribute and forced labour; he fled into the mountains and died there. The chiefdom vanished with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a> collapse.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 1200 CE – 1497 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:83.58%;width:5.91%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1200 CE – 1497 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Guacanagarí</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Taíno religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Taíno</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Marién, Marien</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1348&amp;focus=cacicazgo-of-marien" aria-label="Cacicazgo of Marién on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2724 1468 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cacicazgo of Marién lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4910 989h0M3105 1749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3044 1724h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M3044 1724h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Haiti. <a href="/atlas?year=1348&amp;focus=cacicazgo-of-marien">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
